public class SynchronizationExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        // Without synchronization (race condition)
        System.out.println("=== Without Synchronization ===");
        Counter badCounter = new Counter();

        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                badCounter.increment();  // Not synchronized!
            }
        });
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                badCounter.increment();  // Not synchronized!
            }
        });

        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        t1.join();
        t2.join();

        System.out.println("Expected: 2000, Got: " + badCounter.getValue());

        // With synchronization
        System.out.println("=== With Synchronization ===");
        SynchronizedCounter goodCounter = new SynchronizedCounter();

        Thread t3 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                goodCounter.increment();  // Synchronized!
            }
        });
        Thread t4 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                goodCounter.increment();  // Synchronized!
            }
        });

        t3.start();
        t4.start();
        t3.join();
        t4.join();

        System.out.println("Expected: 2000, Got: " + goodCounter.getValue());

        // Synchronized block (more granular)
        System.out.println("=== Synchronized Block ===");
        CounterWithBlock counterBlock = new CounterWithBlock();

        Thread t5 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                counterBlock.increment();
            }
        });
        Thread t6 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                counterBlock.increment();
            }
        });

        t5.start();
        t6.start();
        t5.join();
        t6.join();

        System.out.println("Expected: 2000, Got: " + counterBlock.getValue());

        // Synchronized static method
        System.out.println("=== Static Synchronization ===");
        Thread t7 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                StaticCounter.increment();
            }
        });
        Thread t8 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                StaticCounter.increment();
            }
        });

        t7.start();
        t8.start();
        t7.join();
        t8.join();

        System.out.println("Static counter: " + StaticCounter.getValue());
    }
}

// Unsynchronized counter
class Counter {
    private int value = 0;

    void increment() {
        value++;  // Not thread-safe!
    }

    int getValue() {
        return value;
    }
}

// Synchronized method
class SynchronizedCounter {
    private int value = 0;

    // Only one thread can access this at a time
    synchronized void increment() {
        value++;  // Thread-safe!
    }

    synchronized int getValue() {
        return value;
    }
}

// Synchronized block
class CounterWithBlock {
    private int value = 0;
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    void increment() {
        // Synchronize only the critical section
        synchronized (lock) {
            value++;
        }
    }

    int getValue() {
        synchronized (lock) {
            return value;
        }
    }
}

// Static synchronization
class StaticCounter {
    private static int value = 0;

    static synchronized void increment() {
        value++;
    }

    static int getValue() {
        return value;
    }
}

phase2.brokenCode:
public class Test {
    private static int counter = 0;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
                // Forgetting to synchronize
                counter++;
            }
        });
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
                counter++;
            }
        });

        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        t1.join();
        t2.join();

        System.out.println("Counter: " + counter);  // Not 200000!
    }
}

phase2.explanation:
No exception — [[warn:but "Expected: 2000, Got:" prints something else]], and it changes from run to run. That's the fingerprint of a race condition.

value++ isn't atomic — it's read, then add one, then write back, three separate steps. [[key:if thread A reads the value right after thread B reads it but before B writes its update back, both threads increment from the same stale number, and one increment is silently lost]]. Do that dozens of times across 2000 total increments and the final count comes up short by however many collisions happened.

The fix is synchronized on the method that does the read-modify-write:

synchronized void increment() {
    value++;
}

Now only one thread can be inside increment() at a time — the second thread waits for the first to finish its full read-add-write sequence before it gets a turn, which is exactly what guarantees no increment gets lost.

